INTRODUCTION\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Axion Fabric v.24 is a next-generation software framework designed to solve the critical imbalance between memory latency and computational throughput. While modern computing has leaned heavily toward GPU-centric models, Axion reclaims the potential of the CPU through heterogeneous core management (P/E-cores), hardware matrix extensions (AMX, SME), and low-latency memory primitives. This version (24.3.0) introduces advanced fault tolerance for cluster-wide RDMA operations and a sophisticated GPU-orchestration layer for hybrid CPU-GPU workloads.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
CORE MEMORY ARCHITECTURE (ALIGNEDSTORAGE)\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Performance begins at the memory sub-system. Axion Fabric utilizes 'AlignedStorage', deviating from standard heap allocation through:\
A. Huge Pages Optimization (2 MB)\
Standard 4 KB pages cause Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) saturation in large-scale matrix traversals. Axion enforces 2 MB Huge Pages to reduce the page table depth.\
Equation: TLB_Miss_Penalty = (Page_Table_Levels * Memory_Latency)\
B. NUMA-Aware Pinning\
Using the Linux 'mbind' system call, memory is physically anchored to the local CPU socket executing the thread.\
Equation: Local_Access_Latency << Remote_Socket_Latency\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
FAULT TOLERANCE AND CLUSTER RESILIENCY\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In a distributed RDMA (Remote Direct Memory Access) environment, node failure is a statistical certainty. Axion v.24 implements a multi-tiered recovery model:\
A. RDMA Persistent Checkpointing (RPC)\
Axion utilizes a "Non-Blocking Snapshot" mechanism. As data is processed in tiles, the result is asynchronously mirrored to a secondary "Shadow Node" memory space via RDMA Write-Immediate.\
Reliability Equation: P_success = (1 - P_node_failure)^N\
By mirroring tiles, Axion ensures that if Node A fails, Node B can resume the computation from the last verified 64-byte CRC-chunk without re-calculating the entire matrix.\
B. CRC-Triggered Retransmission\
If the hardware CRC32 check fails upon receiving a tile over the network, Axion triggers an automatic Selective Repeat ARQ (Automatic Repeat Request) at the NIC level.\
Equation: T_recovery = T_detect + T_retransmit\
This recovery happens in the background E-core pool, ensuring that P-core matrix units never stall due to transient network bit-flips.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
GPU INTEGRATION: THE "CPU-FIRST, GPU-AWARE" STRATEGY\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 While Axion is a CPU-centric engine (optimizing AMX/SME), v.24.3.0 introduces a Unified Memory Orchestrator for NVIDIA (CUDA) and AMD (ROCm) hardware.\
A. Asynchronous Offloading (Heterogeneous Pipeline)\
Axion does not treat the GPU as a separate entity but as an extension of the E-core logic pool. While P-cores handle high-precision INT8/BF16 matrix multiplication via AMX, the GPU is utilized for massively parallel element-wise operations (ReLU, Softmax, or LayerNorm).\
Throughput Equation: Total_GFLOPS = GFLOPS_cpu_amx + GFLOPS_gpu_cuda\
B. Zero-Copy Peer-to-Peer (P2P) Transfer\
Using NVIDIA GPUDirect RDMA, Axion allows the CPU's 'AlignedStorage' to be mapped directly into the GPU's address space. This eliminates the "Double-Buffer" penalty.\
Latency Equation: L_total = L_compute - (L_transfer_overlap)\
By overlapping CPU AMX computation with GPU kernel execution, Axion achieves a theoretical 100% hardware utilization rate across all silicon units.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
